## A deterministic core

The surface rests on the simulation being deterministic and steppable. The tick
is fixed at `TICK_SECONDS` (`0.125`) and driven by elapsed time rather than by
frames, as `specs/movement.md` states, so an interval of game time reaches the
same state however it was divided into updates. Two more properties make it
driveable from code.

- Render-free core. Game state advances from the elapsed time the game is handed,
  independent of a canvas, of the loop that measured it, and of wall-clock time.
  The dependency runs one way: the simulation reads nothing from the renderer.
  Where the next pellet lands is decided from the board alone, never from
  anything the renderer holds.
- Seeded randomness. The pellet placement runs off a generator seeded by `reset`,
  and the game keeps that generator's whole state in its own state, so reseeding
  and replaying the same calls reproduces the same pellet sequence exactly.

Given the same seed and the same sequence of calls and elapsed game time, the
game reaches the same state every time.

## What the runtime provides instead

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The keyboard and the overlay belong to the runtime layer beneath the game, and
the surface carries no operation for the registered actions or for the overlay.
That layer delivers the actions the game registers from the key bindings
`specs/controls.md` fixes, and it draws and toggles the debug overlay. A scenario
that wants to exercise the controls themselves presses real keys at the page.

The clock is the exception, because nothing outside this build owns it. The
surface is what takes the game off real time and advances it, through
`setAutoStep` and `advance` below.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
The clock, the keyboard, and the overlay belong to the {{engine.name}} engine,
which documents them in the `engine/` directory at the root of this repository,
and the surface carries no operation for any of them. The engine advances the
game frame by frame and can take it off real time, it drives the actions the game
registers, and it draws and toggles the debug overlay. A scenario that wants to
exercise the controls themselves dispatches real key events at the engine's
surface.
{{else}}
The clock, the keyboard, and the overlay belong to the {{engine.name}} engine,
which documents them in the `engine/` directory at the root of this repository,
and the surface carries no operation for any of them. The engine advances the
game frame by frame and can take it off real time, it drives the actions the
player controller reads, and it draws and toggles the debug overlay. A scenario
that wants to exercise the controls themselves dispatches real key events at the
engine's surface.
{{/if}}

What the overlay shows is Coil's, under Diagnostics below.

## The operations

The surface carries `version` (`COIL_DEBUG_VERSION`, `1`), a plain number, and
the operations below. Values are plain numbers, strings, booleans, and `{col,
row}` cells, whose `col` and `row` are the integer cell coordinates
`specs/board.md` defines.

Each operation poses one part of the state, and a scenario is assembled from
several of them. No operation arranges a whole situation on its own.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}

Each operation's first parameter is the current state, `DeepReadonly<CoilState>`.
A pose returns the next `CoilState`, built from the one it was handed, which it
leaves as it was; a reading returns what it read. The headings below name the
parameters that follow the state.
{{else}}

Each operation takes only the parameters its heading names and acts on the live
game the moment it is called. A pose returns nothing; a reading returns what it
read and changes nothing.
{{/if}}

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `setAutoStep(enabled)`

Takes the game off real time, and gives it back. `setAutoStep(false)` stops the
frame loop feeding elapsed time to the simulation, so the game changes only when
`advance` says so. `setAutoStep(true)` returns it to running itself, which is how
a build starts and how it is played.

Drawing is unaffected either way: the loop keeps rendering, so the canvas shows
the state the most recent frame left.

### `advance(seconds, frames)`

Runs `frames` whole frames covering `seconds` of game time, each worth
`seconds / frames`, immediately and in order. `frames` defaults to `1`. Each is a
real frame, the same update the loop runs followed by a render, so the tick
resolves exactly as `specs/movement.md` states and the canvas reflects it.

The simulation consumes elapsed time into whole ticks and carries the remainder,
so `advance(1, 1)` and `advance(1, 60)` both run eight ticks and reach the same
state.

Advancing while the game is still stepping automatically adds to what the wall
clock is already doing.

{{/if}}
{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `reset(state, options)`
{{else}}
### `reset(options)`
{{/if}}

Restores every field of the game's state to its opening value: the `title`
screen with `menuIndex` at `0`, the score and the best score at `0`, the combo
multiplier at `1` with its window closed, `simTime` and `ticks` at `0`, the snake
back to its starting chain facing `right`, no live pellet, and no buffered turn.
`options.seed` seeds the pellet generator, defaulting to `DEFAULT_SEED` (`1`).
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
`reset` also sets `autoStep` to false, so a scenario that begins with a reset
runs on `advance` alone.
{{/if}}

`muted` is untouched; the runtime owns muting.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `snapshot(state)`

A pure reading of `state`, returned as the plain object under Snapshot shape
below. It returns no state, because it poses nothing.

### `setScreen(state, screen)`
{{else}}
### `snapshot()`

A pure read of the state, returned as the plain object under Snapshot shape
below. It changes nothing.

### `setScreen(screen)`
{{/if}}

Sets `screen` to one of the six values `specs/ui.md` names, and sets `menuIndex`
to `0`. It changes nothing else, so the board keeps whatever the other operations
posed on it. Moving to `playing` this way starts the tick running from the board
as it stands rather than laying out a fresh round.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setSnake(state, cells)`
{{else}}
### `setSnake(cells)`
{{/if}}

Poses the snake as the chain `cells`, a list of `{col, row}` from the head at
index `0` to the tail at the last index. The chain replaces whatever the snake
was, so its length becomes the length of `cells`. The next tick advances and
resolves collision from exactly this chain.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setDirection(state, dir)`
{{else}}
### `setDirection(dir)`
{{/if}}

Sets the direction the snake is travelling in, one of `"up"`, `"down"`,
`"left"`, and `"right"`, and empties the turn buffer. The next tick advances the
head in this direction unless a later steering request turns it.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setPellet(state, cell)`
{{else}}
### `setPellet(cell)`
{{/if}}

Places the live pellet at `cell`, a `{col, row}`. A `cell` of `null` leaves the
board with no pellet. The next tick eats it normally when the head advances into
that cell.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setScore(state, points)`
{{else}}
### `setScore(points)`
{{/if}}

Sets the current score to `points`. The best score is not touched, and it
resolves through real play, so a scenario that wants `BEST` to rise drives a real
eat.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setCombo(state, multiplier)`
{{else}}
### `setCombo(multiplier)`
{{/if}}

Sets the combo multiplier `M` to `multiplier`, a whole number in `[1,
COMBO_MAX]`. It does not touch the window.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setComboWindow(state, seconds)`
{{else}}
### `setComboWindow(seconds)`
{{/if}}

Sets the seconds of simulation time left on the combo window. A `seconds` of `0`
leaves the window closed. It does not touch the multiplier.

## Snapshot shape

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
`snapshot` returns:
{{else}}
`snapshot()` returns:
{{/if}}

```js
{
  version: 1,
  screen: "title" | "howto" | "playing" | "paused" | "gameover" | "cleared",
  menuIndex: <number>,   // the highlighted item, 0 on a screen with no menu
  mode: {{#if (eq variant.slug "maze")}}"maze"{{else}}"classic"{{/if}},
  score: <number>,
  best: <number>,        // the best score of the session
  combo: <number>,       // the multiplier M, in [1, COMBO_MAX]
  comboWindow: <number>, // seconds of simulation time left on the window; 0 is closed
  muted: <boolean>,
  ticks: <number>,       // ticks resolved in the current round
  simTime: <number>,     // simulation time accumulated in the current round, in seconds
  dir: "up" | "down" | "left" | "right",
  snake: [{ col: <number>, row: <number> }],  // head first, tail last
  pellet: { col: <number>, row: <number> } | null,
  obstacles: [{ col: <number>, row: <number> }],
}
```

The shape is fixed and every field is present on every screen. `snake` lists the
chain from the head at index `0` to the tail at the last index, so its length is
the snake's length in cells. `pellet` is the live pellet, and it is `null` only
when no pellet is on the board, which is the case on the `cleared` screen.
{{#if (eq variant.slug "maze")}}
`obstacles` lists `OBSTACLE_CELLS`, the fixed course `specs/mode.md` fixes, in
any order.
{{else}}
`obstacles` is empty, because this mode places no obstacle cell.
{{/if}}

Every field is read straight off the game's state, so what the snapshot reports
is what the game holds. Two are refreshed rather than posed:

| Field | Kept honest by |
| --- | --- |
| `muted` | Mirroring the runtime's mute bit into the state every frame. |
| `simTime` | Accumulating the delta time of every update on the `playing` screen. |

## Diagnostics

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The runtime's debug overlay shows the values the game registers with it as
diagnostic sources.
{{else}}
The engine's debug overlay shows the values the game registers with it as
diagnostic sources.
{{/if}}
Register at least the current `screen`, the score and the best score, the combo
multiplier and the seconds left on its window, the snake's direction, its length,
and its head cell, and the pellet cell, the same facts the snapshot reports. Keep
each one short enough to read on a line, and keep every source a pure read, so
watching the overlay leaves the game as it is.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The overlay is part of the runtime layer you write. It draws the registered
sources, it is shown and hidden by the backtick key (`KeyboardEvent.code`
`Backquote`), it is off when the game starts, and it reads the game without
changing it. It is a diagnostic layer rather than part of the game's
presentation, so keep it visually plain and clearly separate from the HUD.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Registering those values is the whole of Coil's part, through
`InitApi.diagnostics`. Each source is called with the state current at the read,
as `DeepReadonly<CoilState>`, and reads off that argument. Drawing the panel,
toggling it, and keeping it read-only are the engine's.
{{else}}
Registering those values is the whole of Coil's part, through
`world.diagnostics` in the game mode's `beginPlay`. Each source is a function of
no arguments, called at each read; read the live state at the call, through the
world the mode holds, so the panel reports the frame being drawn. Drawing the
panel and toggling it are the engine's.
{{/if}}
